            "creator": "kevinushey",
            "creation_time": "2026-08-27T04:23:16Z",
            "text": "Created attachment 3802\nCandidate patch.\n\n## Summary\n\nOn recent Windows systems, R uses UCRT with UTF-8 as the native encoding.\nThe narrow `setlocale()` result is consequently encoded as UTF-8.  Windows\nlocale names are not necessarily ASCII; for example, the canonical name of\nthe Norwegian Bokmål locale can contain `Bokmål`.\n\n`Sys.getlocale()` returns such a name with UTF-8 bytes but does not declare\nthe returned R string as UTF-8.  `Encoding()` therefore reports `\"unknown\"`.\nThe return value of `Sys.setlocale()` is constructed in the same way and has\nthe same issue.\n\n## Reproduction\n\nRun this on a recent Windows system where `l10n_info()[[\"UTF-8\"]]` is true:\n\n```\nlocal({\n    old <- Sys.getlocale(\"LC_TIME\")\n    on.exit(Sys.setlocale(\"LC_TIME\", old))\n\n    set <- Sys.setlocale(\"LC_TIME\", \"Norwegian_Norway.UTF-8\")\n    current <- Sys.getlocale(\"LC_TIME\")\n\n    print(list(\n        set_value = set,\n        set_encoding = Encoding(set),\n        get_value = current,\n        get_encoding = Encoding(current),\n        get_raw = charToRaw(current),\n        l10n = l10n_info()\n    ))\n})\n```\n\nThe exact canonical spelling can vary with the Windows version.  An affected\nresult contains a non-ASCII spelling such as one of:\n\n```\nNorwegian (Bokmål)_Norway.utf8\nNorwegian Bokmål_Norway.utf8\n```\n\nThe UTF-8 representation of `å` includes the bytes `c3 a5`, but the current\nresult is:\n\n```\nEncoding(current)\n# [1] \"unknown\"\n```\n\nThe same is true of the value returned by `Sys.setlocale()`.\n\n## Expected behavior\n\nFor a non-ASCII locale name returned as UTF-8, both functions should return\na string for which:\n\n```\nEncoding(current)\n# [1] \"UTF-8\"\n```\n\nASCII locale names should remain unmarked, in accordance with R's existing\nrule that ASCII strings never carry an encoding declaration.\n\n## Cause\n\n`do_getlocale()` in `src/main/platform.c` obtains a narrow string from\n`setlocale()` and wraps it with `mkString()`:\n\n```\nif (cat != NA_INTEGER) p = setlocale(cat, NULL);\nreturn mkString(p ? p : \"\");\n```\n\n`mkString()` uses `mkChar()` and therefore records the result as\n`CE_NATIVE`, even when R knows that the native locale is UTF-8.  The return\npath in `do_setlocale()` similarly uses `mkChar(p)`.\n\nThe adjacent comment, `Locale specs are always ASCII`, is not valid for\nWindows locale names such as Norwegian Bokmål.  UCRT can construct legacy\nlocale names from the English language and country names supplied by the\nWindows locale APIs, and those names can contain non-ASCII characters.\n\nThis often remains unnoticed while the process stays in a UTF-8 locale,\nbecause an unmarked string is interpreted in the current native encoding.\nHowever, the fact that the bytes are known to be UTF-8 is lost.  This can\nmatter if the value outlives a subsequent locale change or is processed by\ncode whose encoding propagation depends on an explicit declaration.\n\n## Proposed fix\n\nConstruct locale results with `mkCharCE(..., CE_UTF8)` whenever\n`utf8locale` is true.  `mkCharCE()` already removes the declaration for\nASCII-only strings, so the change only affects non-ASCII results.\n\nFor `do_setlocale()`, copy the CRT-owned result before calling\n`R_check_locale()`, since a later `setlocale()` call may invalidate the\npointer.  After `R_check_locale()` has updated `utf8locale`, construct the\nR string using the new locale's encoding.\n\nA regression test can select `Norwegian_Norway.UTF-8` on Windows and check\nthe encoding of the `Sys.setlocale()` and `Sys.getlocale()` results when\nUCRT expands the alias to a non-ASCII canonical name.  The test should be\nconditional because that locale may not be available on every Windows\ninstallation.\n\n## Version information\n\nThe issue is present in current R-devel source as of 2026-08-26.  It is\nrelevant to UCRT-based Windows builds using UTF-8 as the native encoding,\nincluding the default configuration on recent Windows versions.",
            "bug_id": 19143,
            "is_markdown": true,
            "count": 0
          },
          {
            "creation_time": "2026-08-27T13:18:25Z",
            "creator": "ikrylov",
            "is_markdown": true,
            "count": 1,
            "bug_id": 19143,
            "text": "Could you please share your `sessionInfo()`? My Windows machines seem to preserve the locale name I give them.\n\nWhat is the underlying issue here? What is broken by having a UTF-8 string being marked as `CE_NATIVE` when the native encoding is UTF-8? R is full of such strings. `charIsUTF8()` is true for them. Both `translateChar()` and `translateCharUTF8()` return the underlying buffer.\n\nMeanwhile, even latest R can still run on legacy Windows systems with an 8-bit codepage instead of UTF-8, where the non-ASCII encoding name must be marked as `CE_NATIVE`:\n\n```\n> Sys.setlocale(\"LC_TIME\", \"Norwegian Bokmål_Norway\") |> tools::showNonASCII()\n1: Norwegian Bokm<e5>l_Norway.1252\n> sessionInfo()\nR Under development (unstable) (2026-07-19 r90279 ucrt)\nPlatform: x86_64-w64-mingw32/x64\nRunning under: Windows Server 2022 x64 (build 20348)\n\nMatrix products: default\n  LAPACK version 3.12.1\n\nlocale:\n[1] LC_COLLATE=English_United States.1252 \n[2] LC_CTYPE=English_United States.1252   \n[3] LC_MONETARY=English_United States.1252\n[4] LC_NUMERIC=C                          \n[5] LC_TIME=Norwegian Bokmål_Norway.1252  \n\ntime zone: Europe/Moscow\ntzcode source: internal\n\nattached base packages:\n[1] stats     graphics  grDevices utils     datasets  methods   base     \n\nloaded via a namespace (and not attached):\n[1] compiler_4.7.0 tools_4.7.0   \n```\n\nWhat needs to be fixed for non-ASCII locale names to work is `CHAR(STRING_ELT(locale, 0))` (it must be `translateChar(STRING_ELT(locale, 0))`, subject to the chicken-and-egg problem of what's valid in the current encoding); otherwise non-ASCII locale names won't work unless the string contents happen to coincide with the ANSI/UTF-8 encoding expected by the CRT.",
            "attachment_id": {},
            "tags": [],
            "is_private": false,
            "id": 103482,
            "time": "2026-08-27T13:18:25Z"
          },
          {
            "is_private": false,
            "id": 103483,
            "time": "2026-08-27T15:34:43Z",
            "tags": [],
