            "is_markdown": true,
            "count": 2,
            "bug_id": 19143,
            "text": "Here's an example; running RGui.exe with R 4.6.1.\n\n```\n> sessionInfo()\nR version 4.6.1 (2026-06-24 ucrt)\nPlatform: x86_64-w64-mingw32/x64\nRunning under: Windows 11 x64 (build 29634)\n\nMatrix products: default\n  LAPACK version 3.12.1\n\nlocale:\n[1] LC_COLLATE=English_United States.utf8  LC_CTYPE=English_United States.utf8   \n[3] LC_MONETARY=English_United States.utf8 LC_NUMERIC=C                          \n[5] LC_TIME=English_United States.utf8    \n\ntime zone: America/Phoenix\ntzcode source: internal\n\nattached base packages:\n[1] stats     graphics  grDevices utils     datasets  methods   base     \n\nloaded via a namespace (and not attached):\n[1] compiler_4.6.1\n> Sys.setlocale(\"LC_CTYPE\", \"Norwegian Bokmål_Norway.utf8\")\n[1] \"Norwegian Bokmål_Norway.utf8\"\n> before <- Sys.getlocale(\"LC_CTYPE\")\n> Sys.setlocale(\"LC_CTYPE\", \"C\")\n[1] \"C\"\n> Sys.setlocale(\"LC_CTYPE\", before)\n[1] \"\"\nWarning message:\nIn Sys.setlocale(\"LC_CTYPE\", before) :\n  OS reports request to set locale to \"Norwegian BokmÃ¥l_Norway.utf8\" cannot be honored\n```\n\nSetting the locale in this way and resetting it is common when e.g. attempting to sort using the C locale.",
            "creation_time": "2026-08-27T15:34:43Z",
            "creator": "kevinushey"
          },
          {
            "attachment_id": {},
            "tags": [],
            "is_private": false,
            "time": "2026-08-27T16:18:09Z",
            "id": 103484,
            "creator": "ikrylov",
            "creation_time": "2026-08-27T16:18:09Z",
            "count": 3,
            "is_markdown": true,
            "text": "Thank you for the example! Now I understand, the problem with `CE_NATIVE` is that the string stops being valid after the locale is changed. Does this work for you on Windows 11?\n\n```\n> l <- 'Norwegian Bokmål_Norway.utf8'\n> Encoding(l)\n[1] \"UTF-8\"\n> Sys.setlocale('LC_CTYPE', l)\n[1] \"Norwegian Bokmål_Norway.utf8\"\n> Sys.setlocale('LC_CTYPE', 'C')\n[1] \"C\"\n> enc2native(l)\n[1] \"Norwegian Bokm<U+00E5>l_Norway.utf8\"\n> Sys.setlocale('LC_CTYPE', l)\n[1] \"\"\nWarning message:\nIn Sys.setlocale(\"LC_CTYPE\", l) :\n  OS reports request to set locale to \"Norwegian BokmÃ¥l_Norway.utf8\" cannot be honored\n```\n\nAn experiment shows that I need `Norwegian Bokm\\xe5l_Norway.utf8` instead of `Norwegian Bokm\\xc3\\xa5l_Norway.utf8` to get back from `C`, so marking the string as UTF-8 doesn't help me:\n\n```\n#include <locale.h>\n#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n\tconst char *bokmaal = \"Norwegian_Norway.UTF-8\";\n\tconst char *bokmaal_utf8 = \"Norwegian Bokm\\xc3\\xa5l_Norway.utf8\";\n\tconst char *bokmaal_latin1 = \"Norwegian Bokm\\xe5l_Norway.utf8\";\n\t#define p(x) printf(\"%s => %s\\n\", #x, x)\n\tp(setlocale(LC_ALL, \"\"));\n\tp(setlocale(LC_CTYPE, bokmaal));\n\tp(setlocale(LC_CTYPE, bokmaal_utf8));\n\tp(setlocale(LC_CTYPE, \"C\"));\n\tp(setlocale(LC_CTYPE, bokmaal_utf8));\n\tp(setlocale(LC_CTYPE, bokmaal_latin1));\n}\n```\n```\nsetlocale(LC_ALL, \"\") => English_United States.utf8\nsetlocale(LC_CTYPE, bokmaal) => Norwegian_Norway.utf8\nsetlocale(LC_CTYPE, bokmaal_utf8) => Norwegian Bokmål_Norway.utf8\nsetlocale(LC_CTYPE, \"C\") => C\nsetlocale(LC_CTYPE, bokmaal_utf8) => (null)\nsetlocale(LC_CTYPE, bokmaal_latin1) => Norwegian Bokmål_Norway.utf8\n```\n\nDoes Windows 11 (unlike Windows Server 2022 here) actually understand UTF-8 locale names while running in the `C` locale?",
            "bug_id": 19143
          },
          {
            "text": "Hmm...\n\n```\n> l <- 'Norwegian Bokmål_Norway.utf8'\n> \n> Encoding(l)\n[1] \"UTF-8\"\n> Sys.setlocale('LC_CTYPE', l)\n[1] \"Norwegian Bokmål_Norway.utf8\"\n> Sys.setlocale('LC_CTYPE', 'C')\n[1] \"C\"\n> enc2native(l)\n[1] \"Norwegian Bokm<U+00E5>l_Norway.utf8\"\n> Sys.setlocale('LC_CTYPE', l)\nWarning in Sys.setlocale(\"LC_CTYPE\", l) :\n  OS reports request to set locale to \"Norwegian BokmÃ¥l_Norway.utf8\" cannot be honored\n[1] \"\"\n> \n> l <- 'Norwegian Bokmål_Norway.utf8'\n> \n> Encoding(l)\n[1] \"unknown\"\n> Sys.setlocale('LC_CTYPE', l)\n[1] \"Norwegian Bokmål_Norway.utf8\"\n> Sys.setlocale('LC_CTYPE', 'C')\n[1] \"C\"\n> enc2native(l)\n[1] \"Norwegian Bokm\\345l_Norway.utf8\"\n> Sys.setlocale('LC_CTYPE', l)\n[1] \"Norwegian Bokmål_Norway.utf8\"\n```\n\nBut at the heart of this, R extracts the requested locale (in do_setlocale) with:\n\n\tconst char *l = CHAR(STRING_ELT(locale, 0));\n\nand IIUC that will do the wrong thing if the locale itself isn't in the (current) native encoding. I guess there are two ways forward: translate that locale to the native encoding before use, or use the \"wide\" APIs for setting and getting the locale on Windows.",
            "bug_id": 19143,
            "count": 4,
